  "_commit": {
    "id": "8dc62b1b832d17661e62138cc6d5441bdeed94ae",
    "author": "Patrick Aboyoun <aboyoun.patrick@gene.com>",
    "committer": "Patrick Aboyoun <aboyoun.patrick@gene.com>",
    "message": "Leave stored NA values out of the count in margin means and variances (0.99.16)\n\nWith na.rm = TRUE, rowMeans, colMeans, rowVars, colVars, rowSds and\ncolSds dropped stored NA values from the sum but not from the count. A\nmean was divided by too many elements, and a variance used the full\nextent of the margin, so the results disagreed with matrixStats whenever\nthe data held an NA.\n\n- Add .nonMissing(), the number of elements of a margin that are not NA:\n  the extent minus the stored rows whose value is NA. The means and\n  variances divide by it, and the variance counts the stored non-NA\n  values with count(y) instead of n().\n- A sum with no value left is coalesced to 0, so a margin with nothing\n  left gives 0/0 = NaN for the mean, as base R does.\n- A variance over fewer than two values is NA. DuckDB returns NaN for\n  0/0, which made an all-NA margin come back as NaN where matrixStats\n  gives NA.\n- The inner subtraction is parenthesized explicitly, since dbplyr drops\n  the parentheses of a nested subtraction.\n\nBuilds on the proposal in #2 (count the non-NA values in the\ndenominator), and adds the guard for margins with too few values that\nthe proposal lacked.\n\nTests compare the six functions with matrixStats on matrices with\nstored NA values, an all-NA margin, and a margin with a single value,\nwith NA and NaN compared exactly. Both new tests fail on the previous\ncode. Suite: 3486 expectations, 0 failures at 1 and 8 threads.\n",
    "time": 1795241649
  },
